.th REGEX III 5/31/77
.sh NAME
regcmp, regex \*- compile and execute regular expressions
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd "char *regcmp(string1[,string2,...],0);"
.br
.bd "char *string1, *string2, ...;"
.s3
.bd "char *regex(re,subject[,ret0,...]);"
.br
.bd "char *re, *subject, *ret0, ...;"
.br
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Regcmp
compiles a regular expression and returns a pointer to the compiled form.
The regular expression is the concatenation of
.it "string1, string2,"
etc.
.it Alloc(III)
is used to create space for the vector.
It is the user's responsibility to free unneeded space so allocated.
A zero return from
.it regcmp
indicates an incorrect argument.
.it Regcmp(I)
has been written to generally preclude the need
for this routine at execution time.
.s3
.it Regex
executes a compiled pattern (\fIre\fP) against the \fIsubject\fP string.
Additional arguments are passed to receive values back.
.it Regex
returns zero on failure or a pointer to the next unmatched character on success.
A global character pointer
\fI\_\_loc1\fP
points to where the match began.
.it Regcmp
and
.it regex
were mostly borrowed from the editor,
.it ed\^\c
(I);
however, the syntax and semantics have been changed slightly.
.lp +35 34
.s3
\fIsymbols	meaning\fP
.s3
.lp +15 13
[\*|]\*|*\fB.\fP^	These symbols retain their current meaning.
.s3
.lp +15 13
$	Matches the end of the string; `\\n' matches the newline.
.s3
.lp +15 13
\*-	Within brackets the minus means
.it through.
For example,
[a\*-z] is equivalent to [abcd \fB.\*|.\*|.\fP xyz].
The `\*-' can
appear as itself only if used as the last or
first character.
For example, the character class expression
\*|[\*|\*|]\*|\*-\*|]\*| matches the characters `\*|]\*|'
and `\*-'.
.s3
.lp +15 13
+	A regular expression followed by `+' means
.it "one or more times."
For example, [0\*-9]+ is equivalent to [0\*-9][0\*-9]*
.s3
.lp +15 13
\fI{m}\fP
.lp +15 13
\fI{m,}\fP
.lp +15 13
\fI{m,u}\fP	Integer values enclosed in {\*|} indicate the
number of times the preceeding regular expression is to be applied.
.it m
is the minimum number and
.it u
is a number, less than 256, which is the maximum.
If only
.it m
is present,e.g. {m},
.it m
indicates the exact number of times the regular
expression is to be applied.
{m,} is analogous to {m,infinity}.
The plus (`+') and star (`*') operations are
equivalent to {1,} and {0,} respectively.
.s3
.lp +15 13
( .\*|.\*|. )$\fIn\fP	The value of the enclosed regular expression is
to be returned.
The matched string
will be copied into the area pointed to by the
.it retn
argument (see the examples below).
At present,
at most ten enclosed regular expressions are allowed.
.it Regex
makes its assignments unconditionally.
.s3
.lp +15 13
( .\*|.\*|. )	Parentheses are used for grouping.
An operator, e.g. *,+,{\*|}, can work on a single character or a regular
expression enclosed in parenthesis.
For example, (a*(cb+)*)$0.
