.ne 6
.s3
Of necessity, all the above defined symbols are special.
They must, therefore, be escaped to be used as themselves.
.s3
.ne 10
Example 1:
.br
.in +5
.nf
char *cursor, *newcursor, *ptr;
\&\fB.\fP\*|\fB.\fP\*|\fB.\fP
newcursor = regex((ptr=regcmp("^\\n",0)),cursor);
free(ptr);
.s2
.fi
This example will match a leading newline in the subject string
pointed at by cursor.
.s2
.in -5
.nf
Example 2:
.in +5
char ret0[9];
char *newcursor, *name;
\&\fB.\fP\*|\fB.\fP\*|\fB.\fP
name = regcmp("([A\*-Za\*-z][A\*-za\*-z0\*-9\_]{0,7})$0",0);
newcursor = regex(name,"123Testing321",ret0);
.s2
.fi
This example will match through the string "Testing3" and will return
the address of the character after the last matched character
(i.e.,
.it newcursor
will point to the substring "21").
The string "Testing3" will be copied to the
character array
.it ret0.
.br
.in -5
.s2
Example 3:
.in +5
.nf
#include "file.i"
char *string, *newcursor;
\&\fB.\fP\*|\fB.\fP\*|\fB.\fP
newcursor = regex(name,string);
.s2
.fi
This example applies a precompiled regular expression
in
.it file.i
against
.it string
(see
.it regcmp\^\c
(I)).
.in -5
.s3
.it Regcmp
and
.it regex
are kept in the
.bd \-lPW
library.
